{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nOperators who run rebar's gate operations through an OpenAI-compatible gateway (set via\n`llm.base_url`) have that gateway reporting to an observability backend such as Langfuse.\nrebar sends no request metadata, so every gate call arrives anonymous: it cannot be\nattributed to rebar, grouped into one trace per gate run, or tied back to the ticket and\nsigned verdict that produced it.\n\nrebar already reserved the field for this. `src/rebar/schemas/review_result.schema.json` and\n`src/rebar/schemas/completion_verdict.schema.json` each declare `trace_id` as\n`[\"string\",\"null\"]`, described as \"Langfuse trace id when tracing is enabled; null\notherwise\", while `src/rebar/llm/runner.py:259` and `:719` pass `trace_id=None`\nunconditionally. The correlation identity was specified and never implemented.\n\nThe existing OTLP path (`src/rebar/llm/tracing.py:196`, `setup_tracing`) is a different\nmechanism and is unchanged by this epic: it exports rebar's own spans directly to Langfuse.\nThis epic serves the deployment where the gateway, not rebar, feeds the backend.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd one generic, operator-configured request-header surface on `LLMConfig`, delivered\nthrough pydantic-ai's `ModelSettings.extra_headers`, whose values may interpolate\nenvironment variables (`${env:VAR}`) and rebar-supplied per-run values (`${run:trace_id}`,\n`${run:ticket_id}`, `${run:operation}`). `${run:operation}` resolves to exactly one of the two\ncanonical rebar identifiers that open a gate scope — `review-plan` or `verify-completion`.\n`review-code` is deliberately absent: it opens no gate scope, which is precisely why the\nomit-on-absent-substituend rule below exists..\n\nRationale for this shape:\n\n- **One generic dict beats named per-vendor knobs.** A dedicated \"trace header name\" field\n  would force rebar to ship a vendor default, hard-coding one gateway's dialect. A generic\n  dict with substitution carries LiteLLM's `x-litellm-trace-id`, Helicone's\n  `Helicone-Session-Id`, or an internal gateway's own names equally well. rebar ships no\n  vendor header name in code or defaults; vendor names appear only as documentation\n  examples, keeping the feature portable across supported client shapes.\n- **`ModelSettings.extra_headers` beats patching httpx clients.** It is upstream-supported\n  (`pydantic_ai/settings.py`), verified reaching the wire on the OpenAI-compatible and Anthropic\n  paths at the pinned 1.107.2 — though NOT on Bedrock, whose model module references it zero\n  times, a limitation the `base_url` gate already excludes and the documentation records\n  and rebar already has a pure assembly seam at `src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py:338`\n  (`build_model_settings`) feeding `src/rebar/llm/runner.py:567`. Patching transport instead\n  would require touching two separate httpx constructions\n  (`src/rebar/llm/providers.py:295` and `src/rebar/llm/anthropic_model.py:155`) and would\n  still miss non-httpx providers.\n**The load-bearing capability is verified, not assumed.** Against the pinned pydantic-ai\n1.107.2 in this checkout: `ModelSettings.extra_headers` is declared `dict[str, str]` in\n`pydantic_ai/settings.py`, and `pydantic_ai/models/openai.py` reads it at `:1001` and forwards\nit to the client call at `:1034`. A throwaway spike drove rebar's own\n`ProviderSession.model_for(\"openai:gpt-4o\")` with `base_url` set, through `_build_openai` and\n`_RebarOpenAICompatibleProvider`, intercepting at the `httpx.AsyncHTTPTransport` seam\n`providers.py` documents: the request reached `<base_url>/chat/completions` carrying both\nsupplied headers verbatim, with the provider's own `Authorization` header intact. So the\nmechanism reaches the wire on the exact path `llm.base_url` selects, and adding headers does\nnot displace provider auth.\n\n- **Static and per-run headers are one feature, not two.** A constant header is a dict entry\n  without a placeholder; per-run correlation is a dict entry with one. One code path, one\n  test surface.\n\nHeader values are a credential-bearing surface: both vendor SDKs merge caller headers last,\nso a caller-supplied `Authorization` silently displaces the real key. Validation and\nredaction are therefore delivered scope, not follow-on work.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nNo rebar-owned root span, no unification of rebar's own OTLP spans with the gateway's\nrecords, and no W3C `traceparent` injection. The 32-hex lowercase id format is chosen so\nlater work can carry a real OTel trace id without a schema or format migration.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An operator can configure arbitrary request headers for gate LLM calls without editing\n      rebar source, and no gateway vendor's header name appears in rebar's shipped code or\n      defaults; checked by a test asserting the shipped default is empty and by a test\n      asserting no vendor header-name constant exists under `src/rebar/llm/`.\n- [ ] A header value can reference an environment variable so a gateway credential is never\n      required in a committed config file; checked by a test resolving `${env:VAR}` from a\n      monkeypatched environment.\n- [ ] All LLM calls within one gate run carry the same correlation value, and two runs carry\n      different ones; checked by a test capturing headers across the calls of one simulated\n      run.\n- [ ] A produced gate verdict names the correlation value in its existing `trace_id` field\n      instead of `null`, on both gate surfaces; checked by asserting `trace_id` on a produced\n      plan-review verdict validated against `review_result.schema.json` and on a produced\n      completion verdict validated against `completion_verdict.schema.json`.\n- [ ] A configured header value never appears in logs, diagnostics, or the\n      `provider_provenance` record written into verdicts and sidecars; checked by a test\n      asserting a sentinel value is absent from captured log output and from the serialized\n      provenance record.\n- [ ] The header names `authorization`, `x-api-key`, `cookie`, `host` and\n      `proxy-authorization` (case-insensitive) and any value containing CR, LF or NUL are\n      rejected at config resolution with a single named error; checked by parametrized tests\n      over exactly those names and those three characters.\n- [ ] A header whose `${run:...}` substituend is absent — any operation outside a gate run,\n      including `review-code`, which opens no gate scope and has no ticket — is omitted entirely\n      rather than sent empty; checked by a test asserting the header is absent from a\n      standalone op's resolved headers.\n\n- [ ] The operator-facing surface is documented: the config key with its three layers and\n      precedence, the `${env:...}`/`${run:...}` grammar and its closed vocabulary, the rejected\n      header names, and a gateway recipe covering at least two distinct gateways so no vendor\n      reads as the supported one; checked by grep-based parity assertions over `docs/config.md`\n      and `docs/llm-framework.md`.\n\n## Child decomposition\n\n- A **configuration** child owns the `headers` field, its resolution across the CLI, env and\n  file layers, `${env:...}` interpolation, and the name/value validation that rejects\n  authentication and protocol-injection headers. It delivers criteria 1, 2 and 6.\n- A **run-correlation** child owns minting the per-run identity at the gate run boundaries\n  and populating the verdict `trace_id` field. It delivers criterion 4.\n- A **delivery** child owns `${run:...}` interpolation and placing resolved headers on the\n  wire through `extra_headers`, gated on a configured `base_url`, together with the\n  redaction guarantee at every emission point and the omit-on-absent-substituend rule. It\n  delivers criteria 3, 5 and 7, and depends on both children above.\n- A **documentation** child records the operator-facing recipe and security guidance, and\n  depends on the delivery child."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787719485369859001,"uuid":"efb5e46a-04ed-436f-8c6c-526c5d79e4f4"}